{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 36, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "והעומר עשירית האיפה", :translit "ve-ha-omer asirit ha-eifah", :gloss "and the omer is a tenth of an ephah"} {:he "והעומר", :translit "ve-ha-omer", :gloss "and the omer"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "juxtaposition (semikhut parshiyot): the word והעומר appears adjacent to the closing verses of the manna narrative, signaling a link"}], :frame {:text "Joshua 5:10–12 records that the manna ceased on the day after the Passover offering, when the Israelites ate of the produce of the land; the unleavened bread they ate that day is identified in the Sifre (Piska 71) as grain that became permissible through the omer offering, which Leviticus 23:9–14 requires before new grain may be eaten. The midrashic reading that the manna lasted until the omer was offered is developed in Pesikta Zutarta (Shemini 9:7) and Yalkut Shim'oni (Emor, §651). The Ba'al HaTurim detects this connection from the placement of the word והעומר immediately after the manna passage in Exodus 16.", :sources ["Joshua 5:10–12" "Leviticus 23:9–14" "Sifre Bamidbar 71" "Pesikta Zutarta Leviticus 9:7" "Yalkut Shim'oni Emor §651"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "juxtaposition (semikhut parshiyot): the word והעומר appears adjacent to the closing verses of the manna narrative, signaling a link", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :exodus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:16:49.364097128Z", :model "glm-5.2"}